## 4. Pitch and glide against pyin

pyin on the reference bass stem (11025 Hz, hop 64, fmin 25 Hz) against every note (median f0 over the note body): of 116 notes, 80 are voiced long enough to measure; 78 sit within 20 cents of the score pitch (most at +4 or -6 cents), so the transcribed pitches are right. Two exceptions: the note at 2.64 s (midi 58, level -33 dB, 0.1 s) is measured one octave higher (+1204 cents), and the note at 34.69 s is 56 cents flat; both are short and quiet. The large "start" offsets seen at 10.26, 17.95, 20.88 and 48.19 s equal the interval to the previous note (1000, 1000, 500 and 1200 cents): they are the previous note's release overlapping the onset, not unmodelled glides. The seven scored glides (`glide.fromMidi`, `ms`) follow the pyin curve (for example at 7.20 s: -276, -116, -26 cents at 27, 61, 96 ms after the onset, an exponential approach as in `tonal_note`). No pitch or glide correction was applied; the 2.64 s octave is left as a known detail.

